  clock: 3  # 4d99
  nonce: 4d99  # 4d99
  message: "Done in PR #2130, on this item own terms: each of the four is either faster with numbers, or has a stated reason. PREMISE CORRECTED: the 815 s figure came from a loaded full run with a recorder attached. Re-timed alone on a quiet machine: stable_id_floor_gate 161 s wall (621 serial), producer_coherence 64 s, catalogue_error_exit_code 39 s, meta_write_discipline 28 s. (1) test_stable_id_floor_gate: a module-scoped fixture (three pipeline runs over ~130 language fixtures) was rebuilt in every xdist worker that got one of its tests; new build_once fixture builds once per session behind an flock. Alone: 161 s -> 42 s wall, one 34 s build where four workers built ~152 s each. (2) test_meta_write_discipline: the live drift scan ran in two tests, now once (-20 s under coverage). (3) test_producer_coherence ratchet: NOT changed; four tree scans, one per axis, ~5 s each unloaded, helper discovery is axis-dependent so nothing to share without rewriting the scanner. (4) test_catalogue_error_exit_code: NOT changed; 175 cases x ~1 s CLI subprocess. SCOPE LIMIT: the gain is for small selections (per-PR CI, local); in the full suite the stable-id fixture was already built once, so the full suite gains about 20 s. No fixed pipeline overhead exists to remove (0.25 s on a one-file dir). RESIDUAL LEADS, not done: both AST scanners re-walk subtrees (5.7M node visits and 792 parses for 396 files in find_discipline_drift); test_route_concept_parity has one 264 s fixture, test_import_dst_ids_are_canonical 81 s, test_doc_family_id_parity 80 s (pipeline work, built once in the full run); build_once could be applied to other module-scoped fixtures that run pipelines."  # 4d99
